From 4bdd79e1d5bf44bdfc80470d360d5463892017c7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rafe Walker <45343084+rafe-walker@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 14:02:11 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 14/17] feat(mcp): add version-checked partial updates for
 nested arrays

Three tools change one element of a nested array without resending the
whole object, where the baseline needed the full value resent (A16):

- insight-update-query-partial: a JSON Patch (RFC 6902, with `test`
  ops) or merge patch (RFC 7396) on an insight's query, optionally with
  name and description in the same write; the patched query is checked
  with insight-create's query schema before anything is written.
- feature-flag-update-conditions: update, add or remove a release
  condition (by condition_id, as the flag read tools compute it, or by
  index) and a variant (by key); variant rollouts must total 100, and a
  variant a condition forces cannot be removed.
- survey-update-question: merge-patch one question by id or index,
  validated with survey-update's question schema; the id cannot change.

Each is a read-merge-write in the MCP layer, the pattern behind the
lost-update bugs, so it goes through versionCheckedUpdate:
1. an in-process lock per object serializes MCP updates of one object;
2. read, and refuse when the agent's base_version no longer matches;
3. apply and validate the patch;
4. re-read just before writing and compare the version marker
   (updated_at / last_modified_at for insights, version / updated_at for
   flags) plus the content of the fields being written; on any change
   write nothing and return a 409 conflict telling the agent to retry;
5. send the API's own precondition where it has one (a flag's version;
   the API refuses a stale one for flags with filters.version 2) and map
   its 409 to the same conflict;
6. for flags, check the version moved by exactly one, and report
   concurrent_write_detected when another write landed in the window.

Remaining race window, stated in each description and the README: a
writer outside this process whose write lands in the one round trip
between step 4 and step 5 can still be overwritten on insights and
surveys, which have no precondition in the API. A unit test reproduces
it on purpose.

Concurrency tests run two simultaneous updates to different elements of
one object, 40 rounds each, against a fake backend with 0-3 ms jitter:
through the tools (one process) both always land for insights, flags
and surveys; across two processes on a flag, every round ends with both
landed, one conflict, or the race reported, never a silent loss.

Receipts are `changed: {path: [old, new]}` plus the new version.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
